18
00:00:25,363 --> 00:00:26,863
Most drops this size ended without

19
00:00:26,663 --> 00:00:27,303
a crash.

20
00:00:28,617 --> 00:00:29,837
But most isn't all.

21
00:00:30,237 --> 00:00:30,897
In this video:

22
00:00:31,017 --> 00:00:32,097
what happened this week,

23
00:00:32,197 --> 00:00:33,277
why stocks are falling,

24
00:00:33,577 --> 00:00:35,257
what history says about drops like

25
00:00:35,057 --> 00:00:35,477
this,

26
00:00:35,597 --> 00:00:36,657
and what it means for your

27
00:00:36,457 --> 00:00:36,897
money.

28
00:00:37,257 --> 00:00:38,117
If that's useful,

29
00:00:38,137 --> 00:00:38,577
subscribe:

30
00:00:39,137 --> 00:00:40,617
we break down money news like

31
00:00:40,417 --> 00:00:41,497
this every day.

32
00:00:42,783 --> 00:00:44,103
Let's start with this week.

33
00:00:44,283 --> 00:00:45,203
On November 11,

34
00:00:45,383 --> 00:00:48,483
the S&P 500 closed at 7,619.

35
00:00:49,163 --> 00:00:49,923
Then it fell.

36
00:00:50,183 --> 00:00:51,123
And fell again.

37
00:00:51,663 --> 00:00:52,563
6 trading days,

38
00:00:52,723 --> 00:00:53,423
6 losses.

39
00:00:54,216 --> 00:00:55,596
Over those 6 days,

40
00:00:55,796 --> 00:00:57,756
the index dropped 5.8 percent.

41
00:00:58,603 --> 00:00:59,403
Today alone,

42
00:00:59,503 --> 00:01:01,123
it fell 2.0 percent,

43
00:01:00,923 --> 00:01:03,403
and the Dow lost about 1,101

44
00:01:03,203 --> 00:01:04,103
points.

45
00:01:04,804 --> 00:01:05,964
To put that in dollars:

46
00:01:06,084 --> 00:01:07,984
if you had 10,000 dollars in

47
00:01:07,784 --> 00:01:09,824
an S&P 500 index fund on

48
00:01:09,624 --> 00:01:10,464
November 11,

49
00:01:10,784 --> 00:01:12,844
it was worth about 9,418 dollars

50
00:01:12,644 --> 00:01:14,564
at today's close.

51
00:01:16,350 --> 00:01:17,250
Quick refresher.

52
00:01:17,510 --> 00:01:19,770
The S&P 500 tracks 500 of

53
00:01:19,570 --> 00:01:20,330
the biggest U.S.

54
00:01:20,130 --> 00:01:20,670
companies.

55
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:23,070
The Dow tracks just 30 big,

56
00:01:22,870 --> 00:01:23,950
well-known ones.

57
00:01:24,150 --> 00:01:26,070
And the Nasdaq Composite holds thousands

58
00:01:25,870 --> 00:01:26,550
of stocks,

59
00:01:26,350 --> 00:01:27,370
with a lot of tech.

60
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:29,680
If you have a 401(k) or

61
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:30,300
an index fund,

62
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:31,660
there's a good chance you own

63
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:32,440
a slice of these companies.

64
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:34,540
So when the indexes fall,

65
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:36,140
your balance usually falls with them.

66
00:01:37,617 --> 00:01:38,977
Here's how far each of the

67
00:01:38,777 --> 00:01:40,577
big three indexes is below its

68
00:01:40,377 --> 00:01:41,077
record high.

69
00:01:41,696 --> 00:01:43,256
Wall Street has names for these

70
00:01:43,056 --> 00:01:43,456
drops.

71
00:01:44,036 --> 00:01:45,336
Down 10 percent from a record

72
00:01:45,136 --> 00:01:46,296
is called a correction.

73
00:01:46,816 --> 00:01:48,236
Down 20 percent is a bear

74
00:01:48,036 --> 00:01:48,556
market.

75
00:01:49,073 --> 00:01:49,793
The Nasdaq,

76
00:01:49,873 --> 00:01:50,973
packed with tech stocks,

77
00:01:51,133 --> 00:01:53,353
is down 13.5 percent from its

78
00:01:53,153 --> 00:01:54,293
October 6 record.

79
00:01:54,553 --> 00:01:55,133
That's a correction.

80
00:01:55,927 --> 00:01:57,867
The S&P 500 isn't there yet.

81
00:01:58,207 --> 00:01:58,767
And the Dow,

82
00:01:58,867 --> 00:02:00,787
which first closed above 50,000 in

83
00:02:00,587 --> 00:02:01,147
February,

84
00:02:01,387 --> 00:02:02,607
fell back below it today,

85
00:02:02,407 --> 00:02:03,927
for the first time since June.

86
00:02:05,417 --> 00:02:06,197
In dollars:

87
00:02:06,337 --> 00:02:08,237
10,000 dollars put into an S&P

88
00:02:08,037 --> 00:02:10,217
500 fund at the October 6

89
00:02:10,017 --> 00:02:12,697
record is worth about 9,178 dollars

90
00:02:12,497 --> 00:02:13,617
today.

91
00:02:14,445 --> 00:02:16,325
But 10,000 dollars put in ten

92
00:02:16,125 --> 00:02:16,865
years ago,

93
00:02:17,085 --> 00:02:18,165
in November 2016,

94
00:02:18,525 --> 00:02:21,605
is worth about 32,888 dollars today,

95
00:02:21,945 --> 00:02:23,085
even after this drop.

96
00:02:23,765 --> 00:02:25,185
Both numbers count prices only,

97
00:02:25,365 --> 00:02:26,045
not dividends.

98
00:02:27,003 --> 00:02:27,823
Same index,

99
00:02:27,983 --> 00:02:28,843
very different feelings.

100
00:02:29,403 --> 00:02:30,783
What matters most is how long

101
00:02:30,583 --> 00:02:31,123
you hold,

102
00:02:31,303 --> 00:02:32,303
not the week you're in.

103
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:34,870
So why are stocks falling?

104
00:02:35,470 --> 00:02:35,950
Reason one:

105
00:02:36,090 --> 00:02:36,750
interest rates.

106
00:02:37,370 --> 00:02:38,650
The 10-year Treasury yield,

107
00:02:38,830 --> 00:02:39,730
what the U.S.

108
00:02:39,530 --> 00:02:40,770
government pays to borrow for ten

109
00:02:40,570 --> 00:02:41,010
years,

110
00:02:41,030 --> 00:02:42,450
is at 5.07 percent.

111
00:02:43,331 --> 00:02:44,251
A year ago,

112
00:02:44,331 --> 00:02:45,771
it was 4.12 percent.

113
00:02:46,458 --> 00:02:47,818
Think about it from an investor's

114
00:02:47,618 --> 00:02:48,058
side.

115
00:02:48,198 --> 00:02:49,878
A government bond now pays over

116
00:02:49,678 --> 00:02:50,818
5 percent a year,

117
00:02:50,618 --> 00:02:51,918
with almost no risk.

118
00:02:51,958 --> 00:02:53,438
A stock promises nothing.

119
00:02:53,618 --> 00:02:55,038
So when safe bonds pay this

120
00:02:54,838 --> 00:02:55,278
much,

121
00:02:55,118 --> 00:02:56,238
stocks have to compete,

122
00:02:56,038 --> 00:02:57,738
and investors pay less for them.

123
00:02:58,950 --> 00:02:59,770
Reason two:

124
00:02:59,870 --> 00:03:00,310
inflation.

125
00:03:01,010 --> 00:03:03,210
Prices rose 4.1 percent in the

126
00:03:03,010 --> 00:03:03,970
year to October,

127
00:03:04,210 --> 00:03:05,990
according to the Consumer Price Index.

128
00:03:06,804 --> 00:03:07,644
To fight it,

129
00:03:07,664 --> 00:03:09,464
the Federal Reserve raised its interest

130
00:03:09,264 --> 00:03:10,744
rate on September 17,

131
00:03:11,224 --> 00:03:13,384
its first hike since 2023,

132
00:03:13,684 --> 00:03:17,424
back to 3.75 to 4.00 percent.

133
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:19,860
Higher rates make borrowing more expensive

134
00:03:19,660 --> 00:03:21,020
for companies and families.

135
00:03:21,580 --> 00:03:23,180
That can slow spending and profits,

136
00:03:22,980 --> 00:03:24,860
and stock prices follow profits.

137
00:03:26,450 --> 00:03:27,290
Reason three:

138
00:03:27,410 --> 00:03:28,870
the stocks that rose the most

139
00:03:28,670 --> 00:03:29,850
are falling the most.

140
00:03:30,470 --> 00:03:32,070
Look at the tech-heavy Nasdaq against

141
00:03:31,870 --> 00:03:32,850
the Dow this year.

142
00:03:33,451 --> 00:03:34,891
The Nasdaq ran up faster,

143
00:03:35,011 --> 00:03:36,631
and since its October record it

144
00:03:36,431 --> 00:03:38,531
has fallen 13.5 percent.

145
00:03:38,331 --> 00:03:40,131
When investors take profits,

146
00:03:40,311 --> 00:03:42,091
the biggest winners usually drop first,

147
00:03:42,211 --> 00:03:42,911
and fastest.

148
00:03:44,550 --> 00:03:45,750
Now the big question.

149
00:03:46,410 --> 00:03:47,490
Does a drop like this turn

150
00:03:47,290 --> 00:03:48,010
into a crash?

151
00:03:48,550 --> 00:03:50,170
We looked at every S&P 500

152
00:03:49,970 --> 00:03:51,730
decline since 1950.

153
00:03:52,510 --> 00:03:53,290
37 times,

154
00:03:53,290 --> 00:03:55,030
the index fell 8 percent or

155
00:03:54,830 --> 00:03:55,770
more from a record.

156
00:03:56,462 --> 00:03:58,382
26 of those kept going to

157
00:03:58,182 --> 00:03:59,342
10 percent or more.

158
00:03:59,838 --> 00:04:01,218
And 11 became bear markets,

159
00:04:01,318 --> 00:04:02,558
with drops of 20 percent or

160
00:04:02,358 --> 00:04:02,758
more.

161
00:04:03,214 --> 00:04:04,854
So roughly 7 out of 10

162
00:04:04,654 --> 00:04:06,634
drops that reached 8 percent never

163
00:04:06,434 --> 00:04:07,514
became bear markets.

164
00:04:08,174 --> 00:04:09,674
But about 3 in 10 did,

165
00:04:09,834 --> 00:04:11,794
so nobody can promise this one

166
00:04:11,594 --> 00:04:12,374
stops here.

167
00:04:13,683 --> 00:04:15,243
Let's look at the two most

168
00:04:15,043 --> 00:04:16,083
recent big drops.

169
00:04:16,663 --> 00:04:17,263
In 2020,

170
00:04:17,063 --> 00:04:18,443
when the pandemic hit,

171
00:04:18,463 --> 00:04:20,723
the S&P 500 fell 34 percent

172
00:04:20,523 --> 00:04:21,483
in about a month,

173
00:04:21,283 --> 00:04:23,123
to a low on March 23.

174
00:04:23,763 --> 00:04:24,903
It was back at a record

175
00:04:24,703 --> 00:04:25,763
by August 18,

176
00:04:25,563 --> 00:04:27,643
and the year ended 16 percent

177
00:04:27,443 --> 00:04:27,843
higher.

178
00:04:28,503 --> 00:04:29,943
2022 was different.

179
00:04:30,243 --> 00:04:32,023
Inflation and rate hikes pushed the

180
00:04:31,823 --> 00:04:34,043
index down 25 percent by October.

181
00:04:34,423 --> 00:04:35,583
It didn't reach a new record

182
00:04:35,383 --> 00:04:37,083
until January 2024,

183
00:04:36,883 --> 00:04:38,123
more than a year later.

184
00:04:38,714 --> 00:04:39,814
For a typical correction,

185
00:04:39,614 --> 00:04:41,614
between 10 and 20 percent,

186
00:04:41,414 --> 00:04:43,594
the S&P 500 took about 7

187
00:04:43,394 --> 00:04:44,674
months to get from its old

188
00:04:44,474 --> 00:04:45,954
record back to a new one.

189
00:04:46,154 --> 00:04:47,274
Some drops heal fast.

190
00:04:47,454 --> 00:04:48,314
Some take years.

191
00:04:49,817 --> 00:04:51,037
What about losing streaks?

192
00:04:51,397 --> 00:04:52,337
Since 1950,

193
00:04:52,437 --> 00:04:54,477
the S&P 500 has had 116

194
00:04:54,277 --> 00:04:56,337
six-day losing streaks.

195
00:04:56,877 --> 00:04:58,277
The last one before this week

196
00:04:58,077 --> 00:04:59,577
was in April 2024.

197
00:05:00,332 --> 00:05:01,512
A year after those streaks,

198
00:05:01,632 --> 00:05:03,472
stocks were higher 91 times out

199
00:05:03,272 --> 00:05:04,552
of 116.

200
00:05:05,281 --> 00:05:06,861
The median gain over that year

201
00:05:06,661 --> 00:05:08,221
was about 13 percent,

202
00:05:08,021 --> 00:05:09,321
not counting dividends.

203
00:05:10,319 --> 00:05:11,499
But 25 times,

204
00:05:11,719 --> 00:05:13,119
stocks were still lower a year

205
00:05:12,919 --> 00:05:13,419
later.

206
00:05:13,859 --> 00:05:15,199
Streaks tell you how people feel.

207
00:05:15,399 --> 00:05:16,599
They don't predict the future.

208
00:05:17,130 --> 00:05:19,750
That April 2024 streak ended with

209
00:05:19,550 --> 00:05:22,870
the S&P 500 near 4,967.

210
00:05:22,670 --> 00:05:24,330
A year later,

211
00:05:24,130 --> 00:05:25,870
it was about 8 percent higher.

212
00:05:27,183 --> 00:05:29,243
Here's something most people don't know.

213
00:05:29,783 --> 00:05:31,703
Big drops happen almost every year,

214
00:05:31,723 --> 00:05:32,683
even in good years.

215
00:05:33,023 --> 00:05:34,683
The red bars show each year's

216
00:05:34,483 --> 00:05:35,183
worst drop.

217
00:05:35,603 --> 00:05:37,083
The green bars show where the

218
00:05:36,883 --> 00:05:37,543
year ended.

219
00:05:38,141 --> 00:05:39,301
Since 1950,

220
00:05:39,561 --> 00:05:40,981
the typical year had a drop

221
00:05:40,781 --> 00:05:42,561
of about 10 percent along the

222
00:05:42,361 --> 00:05:42,781
way.

223
00:05:43,312 --> 00:05:43,812
And yet,

224
00:05:43,612 --> 00:05:46,212
55 of the last 75 full

225
00:05:46,012 --> 00:05:47,292
years ended higher.

226
00:05:48,683 --> 00:05:49,663
One more pattern.

227
00:05:50,123 --> 00:05:51,443
Of the 10 best days for

228
00:05:51,243 --> 00:05:53,503
the S&P 500 since 1950,

229
00:05:53,583 --> 00:05:55,203
8 came within two weeks of

230
00:05:55,003 --> 00:05:56,543
one of the 20 worst days.

231
00:05:57,240 --> 00:05:58,220
The best day of all,

232
00:05:58,320 --> 00:05:59,980
a 11.6 percent gain,

233
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:01,900
came in October 2008,

234
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:03,440
right in the middle of the

235
00:06:03,240 --> 00:06:04,180
financial crisis.

236
00:06:04,883 --> 00:06:06,443
That's why selling in a panic

237
00:06:06,243 --> 00:06:07,143
can be costly:

238
00:06:07,403 --> 00:06:09,043
the rebound often comes when things

239
00:06:08,843 --> 00:06:09,423
look worst.

240
00:06:10,850 --> 00:06:12,610
So what decides where stocks go

241
00:06:12,410 --> 00:06:13,030
from here?

242
00:06:13,350 --> 00:06:14,730
Here are four things to watch.

243
00:06:15,350 --> 00:06:15,550
First,

244
00:06:15,550 --> 00:06:17,010
the 10-year Treasury yield.

245
00:06:17,310 --> 00:06:18,760
If it keeps climbing past 5

246
00:06:18,560 --> 00:06:19,150
percent,

247
00:06:18,950 --> 00:06:20,390
stocks face more pressure.

248
00:06:20,730 --> 00:06:21,350
If it falls,

249
00:06:21,530 --> 00:06:22,130
they get relief.

250
00:06:22,809 --> 00:06:23,429
Second,

251
00:06:23,569 --> 00:06:24,929
the next inflation reports.

252
00:06:25,569 --> 00:06:25,829
Third,

253
00:06:26,029 --> 00:06:27,909
the Federal Reserve's meeting in December,

254
00:06:27,709 --> 00:06:29,649
where it decides whether to raise

255
00:06:29,449 --> 00:06:30,289
rates again.

256
00:06:30,889 --> 00:06:31,449
And fourth,

257
00:06:31,689 --> 00:06:33,189
jobs and company profits:

258
00:06:33,429 --> 00:06:34,729
as long as people are working

259
00:06:34,529 --> 00:06:35,809
and companies are earning,

260
00:06:36,149 --> 00:06:37,609
a drop is less likely to

261
00:06:37,409 --> 00:06:38,469
turn into a recession.

262
00:06:39,950 --> 00:06:41,350
So what does this mean for

263
00:06:41,150 --> 00:06:41,490
you?

264
00:06:41,950 --> 00:06:43,710
Here's what most financial educators point

265
00:06:43,510 --> 00:06:43,990
to.

266
00:06:44,290 --> 00:06:45,430
Money you'll need in the next

267
00:06:45,230 --> 00:06:47,090
few years usually doesn't belong in

268
00:06:46,890 --> 00:06:47,290
stocks,

269
00:06:47,090 --> 00:06:48,670
because a drop like this can

270
00:06:48,470 --> 00:06:49,670
come at the wrong moment.

271
00:06:50,254 --> 00:06:51,454
For long-term money,

272
00:06:51,434 --> 00:06:53,014
time has mattered more than timing.

273
00:06:53,554 --> 00:06:55,754
Spreading money across many investments limits

274
00:06:55,554 --> 00:06:57,114
the damage from any one of

275
00:06:56,914 --> 00:06:57,254
them.

276
00:06:57,494 --> 00:06:59,314
And an emergency fund means you

277
00:06:59,114 --> 00:07:00,374
never have to sell at the

278
00:07:00,174 --> 00:07:00,694
bottom.

279
00:07:02,083 --> 00:07:02,903
Let's recap.

280
00:07:03,443 --> 00:07:05,363
The S&P 500 fell 6 days

281
00:07:05,163 --> 00:07:06,383
in a row and is 8

282
00:07:06,183 --> 00:07:07,383
percent below its record.

283
00:07:07,883 --> 00:07:09,123
The Nasdaq is in a correction.

284
00:07:09,703 --> 00:07:11,443
Bonds pay 5.1 percent,

285
00:07:11,243 --> 00:07:13,303
inflation is 4.1 percent,

286
00:07:13,103 --> 00:07:14,643
and the Fed is raising rates.

287
00:07:15,343 --> 00:07:16,863
History says most drops like this

288
00:07:16,663 --> 00:07:17,663
don't become crashes,

289
00:07:18,003 --> 00:07:18,783
but some do.

290
00:07:19,502 --> 00:07:20,742
So what do you think:

291
00:07:20,842 --> 00:07:21,702
is a crash coming?

292
00:07:21,842 --> 00:07:22,462
Yes or no,

293
00:07:22,502 --> 00:07:23,442
tell me in the comments.

294
00:07:23,242 --> 00:07:25,082
And subscribe so you don't miss

295
00:07:24,882 --> 00:07:25,822
tomorrow's update.

296
00:07:26,494 --> 00:07:28,594
